  "hints": [
    "if the running sum up to the previous element is negative, does keeping it ever help the sum starting there?",
    "what should you do with a negative running total before adding the next element?",
    "at each element, you're choosing: extend the previous subarray, or start fresh at this element - which is better?",
    "track current_sum = max(num, current_sum + num), and best = max(best, current_sum) as you go"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "initializing best/current_sum to 0 instead of the first element, which breaks the all-negative case",
    "resetting current_sum to 0 instead of to the current element when starting fresh"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a list of integers `nums`, which may include negative\nnumbers.\n\n**Find the contiguous subarray of `nums` (at least one element) whose sum is the largest possible, and return that sum.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= nums.length <= 10^5",
      "-10^4 <= nums[i] <= 10^4"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "all numbers negative (answer is the single largest, still-negative number)",
      "all numbers positive (answer is the sum of the whole array)",
      "single element array"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
